      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To report a case of acute bacterial endophthalmitis after antivascular endothelial growth factor injection with a rare presentation of vitreous hemorrhage."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "An 84-year-old woman presented with sudden painless vision loss in her left eye, 3 days after intravitreal ranibizumab injection for cystoid macular edema due to neovascular age-related macular degeneration. The patient was otherwise asymptomatic. Dense vitreous hemorrhage was observed. At follow-up the next day, the patient complained on severe left eye pain. After examination, acute endophthalmitis was diagnosed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Intravitreal injection of vancomycin, ceftazidime and dexamethasone was performed. Vitreous and aqueous cultures grew Enterococcus faecalis. After treatment, the inflammation subsided but it took 3 months for the vitreous hemorrhage to totally resorb. Visual acuity was reduced to light perception."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Vitreous hemorrhage may be an atypical presentation of acute bacterial endophthalmitis occurring after intravitreal injection."
        }
      ]},
